otes":"Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground's OpenStreetMap outlines, not photographs; OpenStreetMap's own outline of the campus (445 × 233 m between Fairfax and the park) and the roads in the ground placed it. Peter Zumthor's David Geffen Galleries (opened April 2026): one long, flowing level, glass all round under an overhanging concrete roof, lifted on rounded pavilions of glass and concrete, crossing Wilshire to a lobe on the south side; west of it Piano's BCAM, a travertine box under sawtooth skylights with its canopy and red stair, and the one-storey sawtooth Resnick Pavilion north of it; Burden's Urban Light on the Wilshire front; the Academy Museum (the May Company building with its gold cylinder, and the glass sphere) at Fairfax; Hancock Park's lawns and trees and the Lake Pit to the east. Changed: Wilshire was 70 m too far north — it now runs where the ground has it, along the campus's south edge, so the Geffen Galleries moved 60 m south (the north lobe also drawn narrower east-west, about 190 m, to sit between BCAM and the park) and the bridge now crosses the boulevard to the south lobe, as the outline shows; BCAM and Resnick moved about 70 m east onto their outlines and Resnick drawn narrower; Urban Light, the palms and the Lake Pit moved with them; the Academy Museum added at the west edge. Guessed: the Geffen Galleries' outline (two rounded lobes and the bridge — OpenStreetMap shows only part of the new building), the pavilions' positions and sizes, the heights (floor at 9 m, roof at about 20 m), the Academy Museum's massing."}